Reading library - the files behind the Ereader app.
(No extension on this file on purpose: the generator ignores it.)

Easiest way in:

    npm run add:doc

It asks for a file, a title and an author, copies everything into the right
shelf, and regenerates the index. `npm run add` has the same wizard under
"Poem / Writing / Book".

Doing it by hand works too - drop files in and run `npm run library`:

    Poems/on leaving.md
    Writings/notes on quiet.txt
    Books/Franz Kafka - The Trial.pdf

The folder name is the shelf. It becomes a filter chip in the Ereader and a
folder in the File Explorer, so a new folder here is a new shelf - no code
change needed.

Formats: .pdf, .md, .txt. Where the metadata comes from:

  .md   frontmatter at the top of the file:
            ---
            title: On Leaving
            author:            (leave out if it's yours)
            date: 2026-07
            blurb: One line for the card.
            note:              (edition, source, anything worth recording)
            ---
  .txt  the filename is the title
  .pdf  the filename is "Author - Title.pdf"

Two optional companions, both matched by base name:

  Franz Kafka - The Trial.cover.png    cover art for the card
  Franz Kafka - The Trial.meta.md      frontmatter-only sidecar, for anything
                                       a filename can't hold (blurb, note,
                                       date). Sidecar values win.

Without cover art, PDFs render their own first page as the thumbnail and
everything else gets the title set on paper. Nothing to prepare either way.

A note on the Books shelf: those are other people's work. Keep the author in
the filename so the card credits them, and use `note:` for the edition or
source if you have it.

Poems are read as one continuous piece; longer prose and PDFs paginate into a
two-page spread when the window is wide enough.
